+## Own view: cross-task seams
+
+- Undo after a multi-seat retirement can restore the wrong pointer (hg-k4l.26 apply-side reparenting composed with hg-k4l.17 undo). `do_retire_seat` reparents one seat at a time against the live overlay. When retiring seats chain into each other, the action records several rows for one holder. Example: TeamspaceRetire or ApplicationRetire retires A then B, with outside seat C.manager = A and A.manager = B. Retiring A records `C: A -> B`; retiring B records `C: B -> nm`. `undo::preview::pointer_restores` loads pointers once and checks each row on its own against the current value. Row 1 (current nm, not B) is skipped with a false "changed since the retirement" warning; row 2 (current nm) restores C to B. After undo, C reports to B instead of its original A. When B happens to retire first, the rows come out right, so the result depends on `list_seats` order. Every test in src/undo/tests.rs covers a single hop (boss_world, lead_world with mid outside the application), so this case is not tested. A fix is to collapse rows per (holder, field) to the first `old` and last `new` before comparing, or to replay rows in reverse against a running copy of the pointers.
+- Docs overstate first-seat behaviour. README.md and CURRENT-DESIGN.md (hg-k4l.20) say "the node manager is the first seat created on a graph" and "the first seat created on a graph that has none is assigned the role". At head that holds only for `seat create`. This is the same root cause as B10.
+- `WhoReply::render` now prints two lines: the identity line plus the manager line. `tests/real_agent_smoke.rs::claude_pane_of` matches a pane when `who` stdout contains the seat name anywhere, so a pane whose manager chain names that seat can now match. This affects only the real-agent tier; no production consumer parses `who` text.
+- NodeManagerSet, ManagerEdit, first-seat assignment and the two `make_plan` guards compose cleanly. The cycle guard sees `seat.create`, `seat.resurrect`, pointer and node-manager effects in plan order. The retirement guard reads the live node manager from the plan tree, and NodeManagerSet emits no retire effects, so the role can always be moved before a retirement.
+- Writer: `StoreError::Integrity` maps to `Fail::Data`, not Infra, so a dangling pointer at apply time fails deterministically instead of retrying forever. Correct.
